EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - MONTAGE OF SEASONS #8#

-- The wheat comes in on the floor; Amos at the flail among the hired men.
-- The first rain darkening the stone around the gate lamp.
-- Rain on the fold; Amos forking extra barley to the ewe with the torn ear.
-- Winter; Eliab's breath showing as he lights the lamp.

EXT. CITY SQUARE BY THE WEST GATE - DUSK, MONTHS LATER

Inside the west gate a caravan is loading for an early start: camels kneeling, bales roped, drivers calling in three tongues. The hot east wind pulls at a cook-fire. Up the street, servants are lighting the city's lamps one by one.

Asher crosses the square with Philon, both in fresh garlands, Jes on Asher's arm, on their way to the night's feast. His purple hem has gone dull with stains. At the edge of the caravan, roping a bale onto his kneeling camel, is the traveller from the village fire. He looks at the garland and the purple hem, and knows him.

TRAVELLER
Eliab's boy.

PHILON
This is Eutychos, old man.

TRAVELLER
The east wind has not stopped in forty days. At first light I go west, by your father's valley. My camel will carry two.

Asher's hand goes to his purse. He weighs it in his palm. It is light.

He looks up the street at the lamps being lit.

ASHER
Tell him the lamps here burn all night.

He walks on toward the lamps, Jes on his arm. Philon throws an arm round his neck. The traveller watches them go, and pulls the rope tight.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - CALF PEN - DAY

A red calf in a pen, its coat glossy. Eliab and SHALLUM (40s), the foreman, at the rail.

ELIAB
Keep a calf on the barley. Always one.

SHALLUM
What feast, master?

ELIAB
The one I am keeping it for.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DAY

The traveller waters his camel at the trough, talking to the hired men. Amos works within earshot.

TRAVELLER
Eliab's boy? I saw him in the city on the plain. Spends like a lord. A woman on each arm. Purple at the hem.

Amos hears it. His face closes.

Eliab has come to the gate behind them. The talk stops. The traveller wipes his mouth.

TRAVELLER
He sends you word, master. The lamps there burn all night.

Eliab looks down the road.

ELIAB
Give him bread before he goes on.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK

Eliab lights the lamp. The dog lies facing the road. Amos stands behind them.

AMOS
Your son is not coming back.

Eliab keeps his eyes on the road.

AMOS
It is written, the elder shall serve the younger.

ELIAB
Read on, son. When Jacob came home, Esau ran to meet him, and fell upon his neck, and kissed him.

AMOS
You never once let me sleep.

ELIAB
When you were small, there was no one else to wake. Then there was.

AMOS
I asked you once. For a kid, for my friends. I was ten.

ELIAB
We had four goats, and a winter coming.

AMOS
I never asked again.

Eliab turns. Amos is already walking away toward the fold.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - FOLD - NIGHT

A drum below in the village, and fires. JOEL (30s), a neighbour, leans on the fold wall.

JOEL
My sister is married tonight. Come down and make merry with your friends.

AMOS
The last of the wheat comes in tonight.

JOEL
The wheat will keep. I will keep you a place.

Joel goes back down the hill. Amos looks into the fold, at a young black kid butting its mother. He looks at it a long time.

Eliab comes to the wall.

ELIAB
The wheat is in. The drum has called you since sundown. Go down to them, son. Take what you will from the fold.

Amos hesitates. He does not look at the fold again. Then he goes, empty-handed, down toward the fires.

